---
import Article from '../layouts/Article.astro';
import { SITE } from '../lib/site';

// NOTE (spec §8.1 / open item #2): Geordie's name and bio must not go live
// until the employment-lawyer review of his Blend agreement is complete.
// NOTE (open item #8): Jesse's bio below is a draft pending his confirmation
// of what is public. Concrete achievements to be added by him.
---

<Article
  title="Who We Are — Erskine Advisory"
  heading="Who we are"
  kicker="Two principals, one obligation"
  description="The principals of Erskine Advisory and the independent technical bench retained on every engagement. Experience from both sides of the table; compensation from one."
>
  <p>
    {SITE.name} is a partnership of two principals, supported by a retained bench of independent technical
    specialists. Between us: years inside the construction industry — including on the builder’s side of the
    table — and the commercial discipline to run an engagement the way a professional practice should.
    None of us takes a dollar from anyone but the client.
  </p>

  <h2>Jesse — Principal</h2>
  <p>
    Jesse leads the commercial side of the practice: engagement structure, client relationships, and the
    standing reporting discipline that keeps an owner genuinely informed rather than merely updated. He holds
    permanent residency in both Canada and the United States and can contract on either side of the border —
    a practical advantage for cross-border families, and for American clients building in Canada.
  </p>

  <h2>Geordie Flanagan — Principal</h2>
  <p>
    Geordie has sat on every side of a construction project except the one he now works for exclusively.
    As Vice President and Partner at Blend Projects, a Victoria, BC prefabricated-home builder, he led sales
    and operations — pricing projects, structuring agreements, and carrying homes from first conversation
    through delivery and warranty. Before that he was Blend’s client: he and his family came to the company
    to build on a beautiful but difficult Salt Spring Island site, under real time pressure, and lived every
    stage of the process from the owner’s chair — including the parts nobody explains in the sales meeting.
  </p>
  <p>
    That double vantage is the point. Owners spend entire projects trying to work out what their contractor
    isn’t telling them. Geordie knows where the room is in a construction agreement because he used to draft
    them. He holds no continuing interest in any builder, Blend included, and works under the
    <a href="/independence">independence commitment</a> like everyone else at the firm.
  </p>

  <h2>The technical bench</h2>
  <p>
    We retain independent specialists rather than pretending to be all of them. Two generalists cannot
    credibly review a structural drawing set or price a change order to the dollar — so those functions are
    performed by people whose profession they are, retained by this firm on the client’s behalf, with no
    relationship to the contractor:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Cost consulting.</strong> A professional quantity surveyor validates budgets, benchmarks bids,
      and prices change orders independently of the party proposing them.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Structural review.</strong> An independent structural engineer reviews drawings and foundation
      design before they become commitments.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Site verification.</strong> Building inspectors in each active region put independent eyes on
      the work before a draw is certified.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Construction law.</strong> Contract work is referred to construction counsel; we coordinate the
      review, your lawyer holds the privilege.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p class="annotation not-prose mt-6">
    The named bench for a given engagement is confirmed in the proposal, matched to the project’s region and
    scope.
  </p>

  <h2>On proof</h2>
  <p>
    This practice is new under this name, and we would rather say so than decorate the fact. We publish no
    testimonials and no project gallery — partly because our clients value discretion, and mostly because
    curated praise is the weakest form of evidence. What we offer any serious enquiry is stronger: the direct
    contact details of every client we have had. Call them and ask.
  </p>

  <p>
    <a href="/project-review">Request a project review</a>, or write to
    <a href={`mailto:${SITE.email}`}>{SITE.email}</a>.
  </p>
</Article>
